From: cidwel <cidwel@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8150-xiaomi-vayu: guard the rmtfs region

Downstream protects the rmtfs region with "qcom,guard-memory", which
reserves guard pages on both sides. That property does not exist in
mainline, so the guards have to be declared by hand.

On vayu it is especially necessary: rmtfs_mem is the last reserved region
of the map and has 1.4 GB of free RAM right below it, so the kernel ends up
allocating memory butted right up against it. According to Caleb Connolly's
note in the sm8150-mainline tree, an adjacent allocation can cause an XPU
violation.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-xiaomi-vayu-common.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-xiaomi-vayu-common.dtsi
@@ -158,6 +158,22 @@
 			no-map;
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * The rmtfs region needs guard pages on both sides.
+		 * Downstream does it with "qcom,guard-memory" on the
+		 * qcom,rmtfs_sharedmem node; here they must be spelled out.
+		 *
+		 * Without them the kernel can hand out ordinary memory right
+		 * next to the region -- and here that is especially likely, as
+		 * rmtfs_mem is the last reserved region with 1.4 GB of free RAM
+		 * just below it. An adjacent allocation causes an XPU violation
+		 * and the modem cannot read its RF NV.
+		 */
+		rmtfs_lower_guard: memory@fe100000 {
+			no-map;
+			reg = <0 0xfe100000 0 0x1000>;
+		};
+
 		rmtfs_mem: memory@fe101000 {
 			compatible = "qcom,rmtfs-mem";
 			reg = <0 0xfe101000 0 0x300000>;
@@ -166,6 +182,11 @@
 			qcom,client-id = <1>;
 			qcom,vmid = <15>;
 		};
+
+		rmtfs_upper_guard: memory@fe401000 {
+			no-map;
+			reg = <0 0xfe401000 0 0x1000>;
+		};
 	};
 
 	vph_pwr: vph-pwr-regulator {
